## P5a — remove the Lakebase graph-DB provisioner and superuser-grant flow

Context: Third increment of `.planning/databricks-decoupling/SPEC.md`. **Neo4j is now
kept** — the earlier plan to delete it is reversed, so `SecretsService.py` survives too
(it exists solely to back the Neo4j "Databricks secret" auth flow at
Settings → Back end → Neo4j). What P5 removes instead is the Lakebase-only control-plane
machinery: instance/branch/database creation and `DATABRICKS_SUPERUSER` grants. Neither
has any equivalent on a generic PostgreSQL server, and Azure Database for PostgreSQL
grants no true superuser at all — `azure_pg_admin` is deliberately not one, verified on
the provisioned server (`rolsuper = f`).

Removing this first also shrinks P2: `BranchLakebaseAuth` (projects/branches/endpoints)
had far more consumers than the spec assumed, and the provisioner was one of the largest.

Changes:

1. src/back/core/graphdb/lakebase/provisioner.py — deleted (906 LOC)
   `LakebaseGraphProvisioner`: created Lakebase instances, branches and databases through
   the Postgres control-plane API and granted `DATABRICKS_SUPERUSER` to workspace admins.
2. src/back/objects/domain/SettingsService.py — 282 lines removed
   `graph_engine_lakebase_provision_result`, `graph_engine_lakebase_pg_roles_result`,
   `graph_engine_lakebase_grant_superuser_result`.
3. src/api/routers/internal/settings.py — 60 lines removed
   Routes `/graph-engine/lakebase-provision`, `/graph-engine/lakebase-pg-roles`,
   `/graph-engine/lakebase-grant-superuser`.
4. src/front/static/config/js/settings.js — 358 lines removed
   The provisioning flow (progress modal, task resumption via `sessionStorage`, live name
   normalisation) and the whole Permissions-tab superuser UI. Verified with
   `node --check` and balanced brace counts; no residual references.
5. src/front/templates/settings.html — 147 lines removed
   "Create graph DB" launch button, the Permissions tab nav item and pane, and the
   provisioning modal. `<div>` (176/176) and `<li>` (11/11) balanced afterwards, and the
   template still parses as Jinja.
6. tests/units/settings/test_lakebase_provision.py — deleted (478 LOC)
7. README.md
   Replaced the "One-click graph DB provisioning" callout with the plain-SQL equivalent
   (`CREATE SCHEMA … AUTHORIZATION`, `GRANT USAGE, CREATE`), and explained why the flow
   went: it needed a superuser that Azure does not provide.
8. src/back/core/databricks/lakebase/grants.py — kept, docstring corrected
   **Mistake caught during verification:** I deleted this file, having concluded from an
   earlier grep that the provisioner was its only consumer. That grep was piped through
   `head` and truncated. `grants.py` is in fact used by
   `LakebaseRegistryStore.grant_app_permissions` (four functions) and by
   `SettingsService` via `resolve_mcp_app_name`. Restored, re-exported, and its docstring
   updated to drop the reference to the now-deleted provisioner. The dangling-reference
   check is what surfaced this.

